winewayland: honour a launcher-supplied Wayland app_id (NEUTRON_WAYLAND_APP_ID)

Upstream derives the xdg_toplevel app_id from the EXE basename alone, so the same
app installed in two prefixes advertises the IDENTICAL id. A desktop environment
matches a running window back to a .desktop entry by that id (or by the
StartupWMClass our generator fills with the same string), so it cannot tell the
entries apart.

MEASURED 2026-09-18: the operator launched Premiere from the pinned
~/.premiere2025 entry and the task manager labelled the running window "Premiere
Pro (Adobe2026)" while the window's own title read "Adobe Premiere Pro 2025".
Confirmed by screenshot. The app was in the correct prefix throughout -- the
desktop entry, the launch log, and every running process had
WINEPREFIX=~/.premiere2025 -- so this is a label and window-grouping
fault, not a routing one. It affects every app installed into more than one
prefix.

Fix: wayland_init_process_name() uses NEUTRON_WAYLAND_APP_ID when the launcher
sets it. `neutron launch` now passes the .desktop basename for this app+prefix
(e.g. neutron-premiere-premiere2025), which is the XDG convention -- an app_id
equal to a desktop entry's basename binds to that entry directly, with no
StartupWMClass guesswork -- and the entry generator writes the same string as
StartupWMClass. Empty or whitespace-only is treated as unset, and with the
variable unset Wine keeps the upstream EXE-name behaviour exactly, so an older
runtime still binds by the fallback.

waylanddrv_main.c had no debug channel; added WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL
(waylanddrv) and string.h for the one diagnostic line. Compiled clean on both
arches.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv_main.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv_main.c
@@ -25,10 +25,14 @@
 #include "config.h"
 
 #include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
 
 #include "ntstatus.h"
 
 #include "waylanddrv.h"
+#include "wine/debug.h"
+
+WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(waylanddrv);
 
 char *process_name = NULL;
 
@@ -59,6 +63,42 @@
     .pOpenGLInit = WAYLAND_OpenGLInit,
 };
 
+/* NEUTRON: per-prefix Wayland app id.
+ *
+ * Upstream derives the xdg_toplevel app_id from the EXE name alone, so every prefix that has the
+ * same app installed reports the identical id -- "adobe premiere pro.exe" for Premiere in
+ * ~/.premiere2025 AND in ~/Neutron-Prefixes/Adobe2026. A desktop environment matches a running
+ * window back to a .desktop entry by that id (or by StartupWMClass, which our generator fills with
+ * the same string), so with the app installed in two prefixes it cannot tell the entries apart and
+ * picks whichever it finds first.
+ *
+ * MEASURED 2026-09-18: the operator launched Premiere from the pinned .premiere2025 entry; the task
+ * manager labelled the running window "Premiere Pro (Adobe2026)" while the window's own title read
+ * "Adobe Premiere Pro 2025". The app was in the right prefix throughout -- launcher, environment and
+ * every process had WINEPREFIX=~/.premiere2025 -- only the label was wrong. It affects every
+ * app installed into more than one prefix, not just Premiere, and it also breaks window grouping.
+ *
+ * Fix: honour NEUTRON_WAYLAND_APP_ID when the launcher sets it. `neutron launch` passes the id of the
+ * .desktop entry it is launching (e.g. "neutron-premiere-premiere2025"), which is the XDG convention
+ * -- an app_id equal to a desktop entry's basename is matched to that entry directly, with no
+ * StartupWMClass guesswork. Unset (a bare wine invocation) keeps the upstream EXE-name behaviour
+ * exactly.
+ *
+ * Empty or whitespace-only is treated as unset. No kill switch beyond that: not setting the variable
+ * IS the upstream path. */
+static char *neutron_env_app_id(void)
+{
+    const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_WAYLAND_APP_ID" );
+    size_t n;
+
+    if (!e) return NULL;
+    while (*e == ' ' || *e == '\t') e++;
+    if (!(n = strlen( e ))) return NULL;
+    while (n && (e[n - 1] == ' ' || e[n - 1] == '\t' || e[n - 1] == '\n')) n--;
+    if (!n) return NULL;
+    return strndup( e, n );
+}
+
 static void wayland_init_process_name(void)
 {
     WCHAR *p, *appname;
@@ -68,6 +108,14 @@
     DWORD utf8_size;
     int i;
 
+    /* NEUTRON: a launcher-supplied id wins over the EXE name (see neutron_env_app_id). */
+    if ((process_name = neutron_env_app_id()))
+    {
+        ERR( "neutron-appid: using launcher-supplied Wayland app_id \"%s\" (NEUTRON_WAYLAND_APP_ID)\n",
+             process_name );
+        return;
+    }
+
     appname = RtlGetCurrentPeb()->ProcessParameters->ImagePathName.Buffer;
     if ((p = wcsrchr(appname, '/'))) appname = p + 1;
     if ((p = wcsrchr(appname, '\\'))) appname = p + 1;
